14. Trusted-head Reset and durable epochs (d1134/d1372). gateway/mod.rs:515
    projects first durable seq per epoch and resets only cursors beyond that
    epoch's trusted surviving head. store.rs:64-175 fsyncs the surviving log,
    allocates max(persisted high-water, observed log epoch)+1 and atomically
    writes/fsyncs/renames/fsyncs the epoch file before admitting a writer.
    Fork materialization writes epoch 1 before publishing. Invalid/unreadable
    high-water state fails closed. SubscriberReplay models content births,
    loss, high-water allocation, reset and a face-prefix safety invariant.
    Real cases: old (1,10) resets to 8 after reassignment 9..11; (1,8) replays;
    current (2,11) is empty; future epoch resets to 0; truncating to 6 starts
    epoch 3 and old (1,8)/(2,9) reset to 6. Bad high-water is refused.
    Red: cargo test --offline --locked --test store_recovery trusted_head_cursors → 101;
    old replay-model nonvacuity command → 101. Separate old-Reset and log-only
    allocator controls each give filtered cargo 101 → 0 after restore.
    Native/Parity reject both BrokenHarnessSubscriberReplay and
    BrokenHarnessSubscriberReplayEpochReuse on safety; correct model passes.
    Evidence: tests/store_recovery.rs:67; tests/formal_nonvacuity.rs:79;
    hardening/item14-* JSON/log files and generated model metadata.

15. Structural runtime gate (budgets §1, 82f2edd/7bdf826). runtime.rs:41 exports
    assert_off_rt_worker(), which requires the private lease established only
    inside spawn_blocking, a held shared permit and a distinct caller thread.
    blocking_hook labels real grep, glob, directory-read, diff, fsync and walk
    entries; stats and temporary observation let tests synchronize real work
    without a scheduling deadline. All std::File fsync sites use this hook.
    rt_budget.rs:60 runs 16 concurrent 10k-file greps through distinct registries,
    forces four-body rendezvous waves and asserts max held=4, all 16 grep hooks,
    17 walk entries and all other core hooks. A worker call without a lease
    panics. Distinct registries prevent their read limits from masking the
    global limit. The old p99 test is ignored outside make bench-budgets.
    Red/green: python3 -I scripts/hardening-controls.py item15-worker item15-permits
    exits 0; its cargo rt_budget command exits 101 with grep forced onto an IO
    worker, and 101 with eight global permits (observed 8 instead of 4); each
    restored command exits 0. The final all-targets run passes that stronger test.
    Evidence: hardening/item15-{worker,permits}.json and corresponding logs;
    bench-budgets-current.json records UNMEASURED, never PASS.

